From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Fri, 17 Jul 2026 15:22:51 +0200
Subject: cc: let a constant method-result capture set be viewed read-only
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`adaptMutability` widens a read-only capture set to conform to an exclusive one by
asking the exclusive (writer) set to become a reader via `toReader()`. A `Var` records
this by mutating its mutability to `Reader`; a constant set cannot, and so failed
unconditionally with a `MutAdaptFailure`.

That is too strict for a constant set whose every element is the existential capability
of a *method result* — a `ResultCap`, or a `LocalCap` localised from `caps.any` whose
origin is a method declaration. Such a capability stands for whatever the concrete
result captures and occurs only in covariant result position, so a read-only result
conforms to it. `Const.toReader` now succeeds for such sets. It remains an error to
widen an empty or read-only capture to a genuine exclusive requirement such as
`caps.any` in a value type ascription (see `mut-widen-empty`), because that origin is a
value, not a method.

This surfaces when a SAM literal over a stateful-returning method is expanded to an
anonymous class before capture checking — which happens under `-scalajs`, whose `isSam`
rejects arbitrary traits — so the override is checked against the inherited constant
`ResultCap`. On the JVM the SAM stays a closure carrying a fresh mutable result
capability, so this path is not exercised there.

Validated against the full `neg-custom-args/captures` suite (223 tests with expected
errors): none is newly accepted.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: samstateful
---
 .../src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureSet.scala  | 23 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureSet.scala b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureSet.scala
index 0f051834e57fbb95be77bc26100f8c7424a4bbe4..880dfe0e1e54956fedf4e462ef86e11265312a79 100644
--- a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureSet.scala
+++ b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/cc/CaptureSet.scala
@@ -632,7 +632,28 @@ object CaptureSet:
         false
       }
 
-    def toReader()(using Context) = failWith(MutAdaptFailure(this))
+    def toReader()(using Context) =
+      // A constant set whose every element is the existential capability of a *method
+      // result* can be viewed read-only: such a capability stands for whatever the
+      // concrete result captures and occurs in covariant result position, so a read-only
+      // result conforms to it. The JVM reaches the same conclusion via the equivalent
+      // fresh `Var` (whose `toReader` mutates it to `Reader`); a constant cannot record
+      // that mutation and would otherwise fail. This is deliberately restricted to a
+      // method-result root — a `ResultCap`, or a `LocalCap` localised from `caps.any`
+      // with a method declaration as its origin — and does NOT cover a genuine exclusive
+      // requirement such as `caps.any` in a value type ascription, where widening an
+      // empty or read-only capture stays an error (see `mut-widen-empty`). It surfaces
+      // when a SAM anonymous class (Scala.js) overrides a stateful-returning method: the
+      // inherited result capability is a constant, whereas the JVM closure carries a
+      // fresh mutable one.
+      def isMethodResultRoot(elem: Capability): Boolean = elem.core match
+        case _: ResultCap => true
+        case lc: LocalCap => lc.origin match
+          case Origin.InDecl(sym, _) => sym.is(Method)
+          case _ => false
+        case _ => false
+      if !elems.isEmpty && elems.forall(isMethodResultRoot) then true
+      else failWith(MutAdaptFailure(this))
 
     def addDependent(cs: CaptureSet)(using Context, VarState) = true
 
